> the taylor series of sin x about 0 to order 5
  ucas: it(x) = sin(x)
  ucas: taylor it order 5
Maclaurin (Taylor at 0) series of it at x = 0, through order 5:
  it = x - x^3/6 + x^5/120 + O(x^6)
  as x -> 0; coefficients c_k = f^(k)(0)/k!: 0 1 0 -1/6 0 1/120
  check: the coefficients agree with Taylor-jet arithmetic (long double) to 0
  check: |f - P| = 1.98e-11 at x = 0 ± 1/10, 1.55e-13 at ± 1/20, 1.21e-15 at ± 1/40 (shrinks like h^7.0; expected >= 6)
> the taylor series of cos x at 0 to order 4
  ucas: it(x) = cos(x)
  ucas: taylor it order 4
Maclaurin (Taylor at 0) series of it at x = 0, through order 4:
  it = 1 - x^2/2 + x^4/24 + O(x^5)
  as x -> 0; coefficients c_k = f^(k)(0)/k!: 1 0 -1/2 0 1/24
  check: the coefficients agree with Taylor-jet arithmetic (long double) to 0
  check: |f - P| = 1.39e-09 at x = 0 ± 1/10, 2.17e-11 at ± 1/20, 3.39e-13 at ± 1/40 (shrinks like h^6.0; expected >= 5)
> maclaurin series of sin x
  note: no order given: through order 5 (say "to order 8")
  ucas: it(x) = sin(x)
  ucas: taylor it
Maclaurin (Taylor at 0) series of it at x = 0, through order 5:
  it = x - x^3/6 + x^5/120 + O(x^6)
  as x -> 0; coefficients c_k = f^(k)(0)/k!: 0 1 0 -1/6 0 1/120
  check: the coefficients agree with Taylor-jet arithmetic (long double) to 0
  check: |f - P| = 1.98e-11 at x = 0 ± 1/10, 1.55e-13 at ± 1/20, 1.21e-15 at ± 1/40 (shrinks like h^7.0; expected >= 6)
> the taylor series of exp(x) about 1 to order 3
  ucas: it(x) = exp(x)
  ucas: taylor it at 1 order 3
Taylor series of it at x = 1, through order 3:
  it = 2.718281828 + 2.718281828*(x - 1) + 1.359140914*(x - 1)^2 + 0.4530469714*(x - 1)^3 + O((x - 1)^4)
  as x -> 1; coefficients c_k = f^(k)(1)/k!: 2.718281828 2.718281828 1.359140914 0.4530469714
  check: the coefficients agree with Taylor-jet arithmetic (long double) to 0
  check: |f - P| = 1.16e-05 at x = 1 ± 1/10, 7.15e-07 at ± 1/20, 4.45e-08 at ± 1/40 (shrinks like h^4.0; expected >= 4)
> the power series of e^(2x)
  ucas: powerseries e^(2x)
power series of e^(2x) about x = 0
  Taylor coefficients c_0..c_7: 1, 2, 2, 4/3, 2/3, 4/15, 4/45, 8/315
  = sum_{m>=0} c_m x^m, c_m = 2^m/m!
  matches e^u: radius of convergence inf
> the sum of 1/n^2 from 1 to infinity
  ucas: sum(n, 1, inf, 1/n^2)
sum(n, 1, inf, 1/n^2)
  converges absolutely: p-series 1/n^2 with p = 2 > 1
  = pi^2/6
  method: partial fractions; zeta(k), digamma at integers
  numeric: 1.644934066848226 (the first 128 terms plus an Euler-Maclaurin tail (integral + 3 corrections); estimated error 4.4e-16)
  check: the closed form and the numeric value agree (difference 4.4e-16)
> the infinite sum of 1/2^n
  ucas: sum(n, 1, inf, 1/2^n)
sum(n, 1, inf, 1/2^n)
  converges absolutely: ratio test: |a_(n+1)/a_n| -> 1/2 < 1
  = 1
  method: geometric series a r^lo/(1 - r)
  numeric: 1 (60 terms (the ratio of terms -> 1/2; Aitken's delta^2 agrees); estimated error 1e-16)
  check: the closed form and the numeric value agree (difference 0)
> the product of 1 - 1/n^2 for n from 2 to infinity
  ucas: prod(n, 2, inf, 1 - 1/n^2)
prod(n, 2, inf, 1 - 1/n^2)
  converges: the factors are 1 + O(1/n^2) (sum |a_n - 1| converges)
  = 1/2
  method: the factors as (n - a_i)/(n - b_i): prod Gamma(2 - b_i)/Gamma(2 - a_i)
  numeric: 0.500000000058105 (partial products to 4097, Richardson in 1/N twice; estimated error 6e-08)
  check: agree (difference 5.8e-11)
> asymptotic expansion of the sum of 1/k
  ucas: asymptotic sum(k, 1, n, 1/k)
sum(k, 1, n, 1/k) ~ log(n) + gamma + 1/(2n) - 1/(12n^2) + 1/(120n^4) + ...
  Euler-Maclaurin: int f + C + f(n)/2 + B2/2! f'(n) + B4/4! f'''(n), B2 = 1/6, B4 = -1/30
  the constant C = 0.577215664901532 = gamma
  check: n = 10: sum 2.92896825397, expansion off by 3.9e-09; n = 100: sum 5.18737751764, expansion off by 2.7e-15; n = 1000: sum 7.48547086055, expansion off by 0
> cos from -90deg to 90 deg using chebysev polynomaial of degree 4
  note: read "chebysev" as "chebyshev"
  note: read "polynomaial" as "polynomial"
  ucas: it(x in -(90*pi/180)..90*pi/180) = cos(x)
  ucas: approx it method cheb degree 4
  note: it has special points at the interval's ends (at x = -1.57079633 it is 0, at x = 1.57079633 it is 0): near a zero, relative error is unbounded unless the zero is exact; keep them exact (costs some error elsewhere)? not by default: say `keep endpoints` to keep them
approx it on [-1.57079637, 1.57079637] as f32, fma
